{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-49928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-9}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-9}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {3}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [1,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49928" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-49928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49928" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\infty }\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{-}.\n$$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-141218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141218" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141212" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x}-1}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{1-\\sqrt{x}}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\sqrt{x-1}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141212" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral II is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [2,4].$\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x-1}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {1}^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141212" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-141212" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141212" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-141212" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  II. ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}\\,$  III. ${\\int }_{-2}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{2}-4}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is not an improper integral since it has finite limits and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in [-1,1].$\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its lower limit is infinite.\n\n- Integral III is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}-4}\\to -\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-2)^{+}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115206" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-115206" "field" "selection" "