ate.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is equivalent to \n${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{{x}^{3}+1}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *lower* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (-1,1):$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to (-1)^{+}\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [a,1]$ and taking the limit as $a\\to (-1)^{+}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-49889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] ["math" "{lim}_{a\\to +\\infty }({\\int }_{-1}^{a}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] ["math" "{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] ["math" "{lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] ["math" "{lim}_{a\\to {0}^{-}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{lim}_{a\\to (-1)^{+}}({\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}+1}\\,\\text{d}x)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-82842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral  ${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-82842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-2} & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{u}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{2}{a}| \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-82842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-82842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-82842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-82842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The integral is divergent"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to {0}^{+}\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{4}{\\sqrt[5]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}4{x}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5{x}^{4/5}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}5\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{4}}{|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{1}^{4}}-5\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =5(1)-5(0) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49894" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-49894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{5}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-49894" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3}{(3x-2)^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=3x-2.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=3\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $4$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2/3}^{2}\\frac{3\\,\\text{d}x}{(3x-2)^{5}} & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{{u}^{5}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{{u}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{4}{u}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{(-\\frac{1}{4}\\,{u}^{-4})|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{1}{{u}^{4}}|}_{a}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{1}{256}-\\frac{1}{{a}^{4}}] \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115353" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-115353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{2}{5}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{8}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-115353" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The integral is divergent"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-6039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-6039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=4-t.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=-1\\,⟹\\,-\\text{d}u=\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}} & ={\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{3}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{3}{u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2{u}^{1/2}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{2\\sqrt{u}|}_{a}^{3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[2\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{a}] \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}-0 \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{\\text{d}t}{\\sqrt{4-t}}=2\\sqrt{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-6039" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-6039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-6039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\sqrt{3}"] ["math" "2\\sqrt{3}-2\\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{3}}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-6039" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-351487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-351487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[5]{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u)={u}^{-1/5}\\to \\infty \\,\\text{ as }\\,u\\to {0}^{+}.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to {0}^{+}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}{u}^{-1/5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\frac{5}{4}{u}^{4/5}|}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {1}^{4/5}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {a}^{4/5}] \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\sqrt[5]{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-351487" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1854f54b1c68a3a529c4fd3761b996338d92dbb779e14197d2a7654fd13665f"} {"question" "q-351487" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "q-351487" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/state.json" "file_sha256" "44ad08f125d93e43bcceb3b87737a6603530712d196f8cf258e91333eaf8fe7e"} {"question" "e-7297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$  II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$  III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/example-7297.json" "file_sha256" "d4eab4f559d68fc14c8da7205576c71de72b3dd381c05381764ea90d1c817722"} {"question" "e-7297" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and \n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+.\n$$\n\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/example-7297.json" "file_sha256" "d4eab4f559d68fc14c8da7205576c71de72b3dd381c05381764ea90d1c817722"} {"question" "e-9959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Rewrite the improper integral $\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}$ as the limit of definite integral." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/example-9959.json" "file_sha256" "4bff909d61b3dc355a1b7d6d5db440abb2c5da8cc746eb08e99842a4808cd943"} {"question" "e-9959" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf(x) =   \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left( \\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}}   \\right)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/example-9959.json" "file_sha256" "4bff909d61b3dc355a1b7d6d5db440abb2c5da8cc746eb08e99842a4808cd943"} {"question" "e-517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/example-517.json" "file_sha256" "6c32f7fa2eeb070979838f43d935accf22f2feb087a706dae57027e893fd12e8"} {"question" "e-517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{-\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{1}^{0}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{ as }\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+.\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{u} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u||}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|1|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to {0}^{+}}[0-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|a|] \\\\  & =-(-\\infty ) \\\\  & =\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043930/example-517.json" "file_sha256" "6c32f7fa2eeb070979838f43d935accf22f2feb087a706dae57027e893fd12e8"}] "usage" {"e-517" [] "e-7297" [] "e-9959" [] "q-6039" []} "basis" 1743 "retractions" [[17592186083745 :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{4}\\frac{dt}{\\sqrt{4 - t}}$?"] [17592186083016 :question/problem "Which of the following is an improper integral of the second kind?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\infty} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x-2}\\qquad$ II. $\\displaystyle \\int_{-2}^{0} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x+2}\\qquad$ III. $\\displaystyle \\int_{2}^{3} \\dfrac{\\text{d}x}{x^2-1}$"] [17592186083016 :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I is an improper integral over an *infinite* domain since its upper limit is infinite. So, it is not an improper integral of the second kind.\n- Integral II is an improper integral of the second kind since it has finite limits, and\n$f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x+2} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow (-2)^+$.\n- Integral III is not an improper integral since it has finite limits, and the function under the integral is finite at every point $x\\in[2,3]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""] [17592186083059 :question/worked-solution "This is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded as we approach the *upper* integration limit from any point that lies in the interval $x\\in (0,{\\color{blue}{1}})$:\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac {1}{x^2-1} \\rightarrow -\\infty\\quad \\textrm{as}\\quad x\\rightarrow {\\color{blue}{1}} ^-\n$$\n\nWe can attempt to evaluate this integral by integrating $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [0, b]$ and taking the limit as $b\\to{\\color{blue}1}^-$:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{1} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} = \\lim\\limits_{b \\to {\\color{blue}{1}}^-} \\left(\\displaystyle {\\int_{0}^{b} \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{x^2-1}} \\right)\n$$"] [17592186082923 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_0^{\\pi/2}\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x$."] [17592186082923 :question/worked-solution "First, let's rewrite the integral using a substitution. Let $u = \\cos x$. Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = -\\sin x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\textrm d u = \\sin x\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe use the table below to change the limits:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nCarrying out the change of variable, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\tan xdx &= \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{\\pi/2}\\frac{\\sin xdx}{\\cos x} \\\\\n&= \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{-du}{u} \\\\\n&= - \\int_{1}^{0}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an improper integral of the second kind because the function under the integral is unbounded at the *lower* limit of integration.\n\n$$\nf(u) = \\dfrac{1}{u} \\rightarrow \\infty \\quad\\textrm{as}\\quad u\\rightarrow 0^+\n$$\n\nNonetheless, it is possible to calculate the integral by setting the *lower* bound equal to some parameter $a$, integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to0^+$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} &= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\int_{a}^{1}\\frac{du}{u} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\ln \\mid u \\mid \\mid _{a}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}\\left[\\ln\\lvert 1\\rvert-\\ln\\lvert a\\rvert\\right] \\\\\n&= \\lim_{a \\to 0^{+}}[0 - \\ln \\mid a \\mid ] \\\\\n&= - (- \\infty) \\\\\n&= \\infty\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the integral is divergent."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186081837 17592186081838 17592186081839 17592186081840 17592186081841] "immutable_fields" [[17592186081836 :answer-field/correct] [17592186081836 :answer-field/id] [17592186081836 :answer-field/key] [17592186081836 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
